content.theory:
Responsive design means your page looks good on a phone, a tablet, and a monitor — without a separate site per device.

### 1. Media queries
The core tool. You write rules that only apply above (or below) a certain screen width:
```css
/* Base: phone styles */
.card { width: 100%; }

/* 640px and wider: tablet+ */
@media (min-width: 640px) {
  .card { width: 50%; }
}

/* 1024px and wider: desktop */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .card { width: 33.333%; }
}
```
Design for the phone first, then add breakpoints as the screen grows — this is **mobile-first**.

### 2. Fluid units
Use units that scale instead of fixed pixels:
```css
.container {
  width: 90%;      /* % of parent */
  max-width: 1200px; /* but never wider than 1200px */
}
body { font-size: clamp(16px, 2vw, 24px); }
```
`clamp(min, preferred, max)` makes text scale smoothly.

### 3. Flexible layout
```css
.grid {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
.grid > * {
  flex: 1 1 250px;  /* grow, shrink, base 250px */
}
```
Items reflow onto new lines automatically when space runs out.

### 4. Images
```css
img {
  max-width: 100%;  /* never overflow its container */
  height: auto;     /* keep aspect ratio */
}
```

### 5. Mobile meta tag
Make sure the phone uses real width (not a scaled desktop):
```html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
```
